    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Here Are Some of the Serious and Obscure Brands That EICMA 2026 Had To Offer – Worth Knowing About\nStarship's Most Ambitious Launch Yet, Flight 15, Ends With a Bittersweet Outcome\n8 photos\nPhoto: SpaceX\nYears of preparation and 14 previous test flights culminated this past weekend with Flight 15 of the SpaceX Starship. It was supposed to mark the first-ever double-catch of the booster and spacecraft in the arms of Mechazilla towers, but everything ended in a bittersweet manner as the mission was only partially accomplished.\nStarship Flight 15 started on Friday, November 13, when launch pad 2 at the private space company's Starbase complex in Texas ignited its engines and departed the surface of the planet.\nThe mission was off to a pretty amazing start, with the Super Heavy booster meant to propel the spacecraft into orbit igniting all its 33 Raptor engines, thundering off the pad just as planned. It successfully completed its ascent and hot staging and then began its descent back to the place it had left only minutes before.\nOn its way down, the hardware seemed to be performing every single task perfectly. It went through the required directional flip maneuver and fired up 32 of its 33 engines once more (not a perfect outcome, but something that has happened before, and is a non-issue) for the boostback burn.\nPhoto: SpaceX For reasons that are still under investigation, but seem to have to do with low fuel mass, the hardware's guidance, navigation, and control systems decided it's not a good idea for the booster to get back to the pad, as intended. They triggered an automated abort sequence as the Super Heavy was coasting, diverting the hardware away from the pad and to an offshore location.\nAs expected, the rocket then executed a landing burn, slowing down its descent above the surface of the water, and, as it was hovering over the Gulf of Mexico, cut off its engines. Finally, the flight termination system kicked in, and the story of this Super Heavy ended in a spectacular explosion.\nBy all intents and purposes, this portion of the flight was a failure, as the booster was supposed to come back to the tower, as it had done three times before. Despite this, nobody considers the event to be a failure, as, if you really think about it, Flight 15 was more about the Starship. And that one performed flawlessly.\nFollowing its separation from the Super Heavy, Starship moved on to Earth orbit by firing its six Raptor engines. It moved through a brief coast stage as it climbed, then fired up a single engine to perform its orbital insertion burn.\nPhoto: SpaceX For the second time in its history, the spacecraft made it to orbit, where it proceeded to deploy its cargo of 26 Starlink V3 satellites. The deployment went without a hitch, and, SpaceX says, contact with the satellites was quickly established, and they began raising their orbits to become part of the constellation.\nOnce this part of the flight was complete, Starship was not left with much more to do, but, as teams on the ground sought to test pretty much all of the onboard systems (engines, comms systems, flight computers, navigation sensors, and structural integrity), it spent no less than 20 hours in orbit – the longest it has stayed up there so far.\nWhen everything was said and done, Starship began its return to the launch pad , relighting a single Raptor engine to perform a de-orbit burn. It then made its way back to the surface, following a path that took it over some populated areas in northern Mexico and southern Texas, but around the major cities of the Rio Grande Valley.\nAs it approached, it performed a final swoosh maneuver and fired up three of its engines to slow its descent. In a perfect return, it flipped and slowly positioned itself directly in the two metal arms of the Mechazilla tower, which grabbed hold of it and secured it for the first time ever.\nPhoto: SpaceX From start to finish, Starship's Flight 15 spent 22.5 hours on the job, and, even if it hasn't ended in a demo of full reusability, it is considered by SpaceX a defining moment that changed everything.\nCatching a spacecraft in a tower is something that no one has ever done before, and effectively puts SpaceX on the path not only to full reusability for the booster and Starship, but also on the path to fulfilling its role in the Artemis Moon exploration program, the stepping stone to humanity's first crewed mission to Mars .\nIn the days to come, we expect Elon Musk's space company to reveal what went wrong with the Super Heavy return and imagine a fix for it. It's too early to tell when Flight 16 will take place, but it's more than obvious it will build on the successes (and failures) of this one, and will try to be remembered as the first time an entire orbital system of this size is recovered in working order. Mechazilla catches Starship! Congratulations to the entire SpaceX team on this incredible achievement! https://t.co/YJzmGUZb2B — SpaceX (@SpaceX) November 14, 2026\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nstarship spacex flight 15 Super Heavy\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Former NBA Star Takes His Mercedes-AMG G 63 to a Tuner, Drives Back in a Different-Looking Ride",
